{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And Laban answered, etc.</b> The saying ״וַיַּעַן״ [and he answered] is a response to his saying ״שִׂים כֹּה נֶגֶד״ [set it here, etc.,] ״וְיוֹכִיחוּ״ [and let them testify]. To this he answered that he has no desire to humiliate his sons and daughters, and he will not stand in judgment with them. And this itself is his cunning — to say that testimony would prove that the truth is with him, except that he is not doing so out of honor for his sons and daughters." "<b>Further,</b> he hinted how he would justify himself in his claim when he says, ״these are the vessels of my house״ — it is obvious that Jacob knew the markings of Laban's vessels better than he did, and the reason is ״for the daughters are my daughters,״ and they are expert in the markings of the vessels and the like, more than I am, because they use them constantly." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to respond to [the objection] arising from his having searched through all his vessels — for on the side that everything he considers as though it were his own, since the daughters are his daughters, etc., and everything is his, he acted like a man doing with his own property; for had he considered him a stranger, he would not have done so." "<b>and to my daughters, etc., to these, etc.</b> The word ״לָאֵלֶּה״ [to these] has no meaning [on its own]. Perhaps it refers back to ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר אַתָּה רוֹאֶה״ [all that you see], and the construal of the verse is as follows: ״וְלִבְנוֹתַי״ — meaning, וּלְצַד בְּנוֹתַי [on account of my daughters] — what can I do to these assets also on account of their children? Or it may intend in this manner: ״וְלִבְנוֹתַי״ — what can I do out of malice? For even though today they consider themselves strangers and uproot from themselves the name 'my daughters,' which is what he means by <b>לָאֵלֶּה הַיּוֹם</b> — meaning that today they are called 'these' in my eyes on account of their deeds — nevertheless they are my daughters." "<b>to their sons whom they bore.</b> He was precise to say ״אֲשֶׁר יָלָדוּ״ [whom they bore], so as not to trace the sons after their mother. Therefore he said ״אֲשֶׁר יָלְדוּ,״ to say that his calling them 'their sons' is not to trace them after them, for [they belong] to their families to their fathers' house, but rather because they bore them he called them by the name 'their sons.' And even though these are the words of Laban, nevertheless the Torah teaches laws through the medium of narratives, as you find that they learned (Yerushalmi Mo'ed Katan 1:7:3): from where do we derive that one does not mix one rejoicing with another rejoicing? For it is written ״מַלֵּא שְׁבֻעַ זֹאת״ [complete this week,] etc. — even though these are the words of Laban."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:45:33.225246590Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}